1778
01:04:35,954 --> 01:04:38,224
They spent a whole 50 days preparing for this?

1779
01:04:38,854 --> 01:04:40,194
- We're fake!
- We're fake!

1780
01:04:40,194 --> 01:04:41,724
I can't believe this.

1781
01:04:42,124 --> 01:04:43,194
(Next up, it's time to set up Haenyeo's House!)

1782
01:04:43,194 --> 01:04:45,494
- My goodness.
- What was this place for?

1783
01:04:46,164 --> 01:04:47,864
(This place used to be a resting shelter for haenyeo.)

1784
01:04:47,864 --> 01:04:49,934
- A shelter.
- It was a resting place.

1785
01:04:49,934 --> 01:04:51,834
- So...
- They remodeled the place?

1786
01:04:52,434 --> 01:04:55,844
(Props from the mainland have arrived.)

1787
01:04:55,904 --> 01:04:59,244
(Filling up the bare space from scratch)

1788
01:04:59,314 --> 01:05:00,644
(Haenyeo wetsuits, framed photos, drinks, and stickers)

1789
01:05:00,644 --> 01:05:01,984
They decorated it so well.

1790
01:05:02,084 --> 01:05:05,084
(The fake TV appearance posters were crafted carefully.)

1791
01:05:05,084 --> 01:05:06,214
Even these?

1792
01:05:06,684 --> 01:05:08,054
They made these too?

1793
01:05:08,354 --> 01:05:12,294
(The fake haenyeo restaurant is complete!)

1794
01:05:13,594 --> 01:05:14,594
I was totally tricked...

1795
01:05:14,594 --> 01:05:16,694
- by the taste of it.
- It was so tasty.

1796
01:05:16,934 --> 01:05:18,494
(The seaweed japguk is made with unmarketable seafood?)

1797
01:05:18,494 --> 01:05:20,404
Is that an actual dish that exists?

1798
01:05:21,364 --> 01:05:22,604
Seaweed japguk.

1799
01:05:23,304 --> 01:05:25,034
Have you heard of seaweed japguk before?

1800
01:05:25,174 --> 01:05:26,944
I've never heard of it before.

1801
01:05:26,944 --> 01:05:28,604
What?

1802
01:05:29,604 --> 01:05:31,774
(Then what is the dish we ate?)

1803
01:05:31,774 --> 01:05:33,814
How do you make seaweed japguk?

1804
01:05:33,844 --> 01:05:35,344
I've never done it before.

1805
01:05:35,714 --> 01:05:37,254
I don't know how to make it.

1806
01:05:37,254 --> 01:05:40,014
(So the production crew invented it out of imagination.)

1807
01:05:40,184 --> 01:05:42,484
We have a lot of top shells. Shall we add some?

1808
01:05:42,484 --> 01:05:43,484
Good.

1809
01:05:44,224 --> 01:05:46,724
- That's unbelievable.
- They're frauds.

1810
01:05:46,724 --> 01:05:47,764
Seriously.

1811
01:05:47,764 --> 01:05:48,824
(The seaweed japguk is complete.)

1812
01:05:49,124 --> 01:05:52,364
- Gosh, this is...
- And we thought it was delicious.

1813
01:05:52,734 --> 01:05:54,404
We said it had a rich flavor.

1814
01:05:55,604 --> 01:05:56,664
What?

1815
01:05:56,664 --> 01:05:58,004
(And the secret weapon for the grand finale)

1816
01:05:58,674 --> 01:06:01,174
They used a major brand's product!

1817
01:06:01,174 --> 01:06:04,844
(We can't go without the classic major-brand flavor.)

1818
01:06:05,144 --> 01:06:06,674
(The secret to the rich broth is instant broth poured right in.)

1819
01:06:06,674 --> 01:06:08,984
(Proud)

1820
01:06:09,184 --> 01:06:10,354
Did you see...

1821
01:06:10,354 --> 01:06:11,714
- how spot-on my palate is?
- I saw it.

1822
01:06:12,514 --> 01:06:13,584
Right?

1823
01:06:14,584 --> 01:06:16,924
I'm Kim Jin Gyeong, specializing...

1824
01:06:16,924 --> 01:06:18,124
in traditional Jeju cuisine.

1825
01:06:18,294 --> 01:06:20,494
No wonder it tasted so good.

1826
01:06:20,494 --> 01:06:21,794
(Even the side dishes were set up in authentic Jeju local style.)

1827
01:06:21,964 --> 01:06:23,224
The bracken was so delicious.

1828
01:06:23,224 --> 01:06:24,734
My goodness.

1829
01:06:24,734 --> 01:06:26,164
(The fake haenyeo feast is ready!)

1830
01:06:26,664 --> 01:06:27,734
Where were the B-Side clues...

1831
01:06:27,734 --> 01:06:28,804
- hidden today?
- Seriously.

1832
01:06:28,804 --> 01:06:30,064
(Today's B-Side)

1833
01:06:30,274 --> 01:06:31,974
- Where were they?
- Where?

1834
01:06:33,174 --> 01:06:34,904
Year 2026 marks the 10th anniversary...

1835
01:06:34,904 --> 01:06:36,744
of Jeju Haenyeo Culture's inscription...

1836
01:06:36,744 --> 01:06:38,414
as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.

1837
01:06:38,414 --> 01:06:39,814
(This year marks the 10th anniversary...)

1838
01:06:39,814 --> 01:06:41,084
(of Jeju Haenyeo Culture's inscription.)

1839
01:06:41,144 --> 01:06:42,314
(Today's B-Side was haenyeo.)

1840
01:06:42,314 --> 01:06:44,414
The haenyeo was today's B-Side?

1841
01:06:44,914 --> 01:06:46,254
(The first B-Side)

1842
01:06:46,254 --> 01:06:47,924
- What was there?
- Here?

1843
01:06:48,154 --> 01:06:49,754
(The unusual certificates of award at the buckwheat restaurant)

1844
01:06:49,754 --> 01:06:51,494
- Right. This was it.
- I saw this.

1845
01:06:51,624 --> 01:06:53,294
- We saw it, but we didn't solve it.
- What's that?

1846
01:06:53,394 --> 01:06:55,524
(Suspicious records...)

1847
01:06:55,664 --> 01:06:58,764
(written on the certificates)

1848
01:06:58,934 --> 01:07:01,964
I guess those are diving records for how deep a haenyeo can go.

1849
01:07:02,134 --> 01:07:04,434
(Records related to haenyeo?)

1850
01:07:04,774 --> 01:07:07,204
(Speed reading: 120 seconds)

1851
01:07:08,444 --> 01:07:11,074
(The duration a haenyeo holds her breath in a single dive)

1852
01:07:11,674 --> 01:07:12,674
It's 120 seconds.

1853
01:07:12,674 --> 01:07:15,514
- Holding breath for two minutes?
- In a single dive?

1854
01:07:16,144 --> 01:07:18,484
(Bungee jump: 15m)

1855
01:07:18,784 --> 01:07:21,024
(The average diving depth of a haenyeo)

1856
01:07:21,924 --> 01:07:23,154
Up to 15m?

1857
01:07:23,484 --> 01:07:24,754
They go that deep.

1858
01:07:24,754 --> 01:07:26,994
Diving 15m down on a single breath is insane.

1859
01:07:26,994 --> 01:07:29,124
(Completed climb: 255 minutes)

1860
01:07:29,624 --> 01:07:32,364
(The average daily diving time of a haenyeo: 255 minutes)

1861
01:07:32,364 --> 01:07:33,834
The daily diving time?

1862
01:07:34,534 --> 01:07:35,534
Awesome.

1863
01:07:36,164 --> 01:07:38,204
It's really admirable.

1864
01:07:38,204 --> 01:07:39,434
(The truly admirable lives of the haenyeo)

1865
01:07:39,604 --> 01:07:40,944
(Today's second B-Side)

1866
01:07:40,944 --> 01:07:42,444
I saw that.

1867
01:07:42,644 --> 01:07:44,144
- We saw that.
- What?

1868
01:07:44,474 --> 01:07:45,814
So Min brought it earlier.

1869
01:07:47,314 --> 01:07:48,384
What?

1870
01:07:48,384 --> 01:07:49,714
(What's the story behind these photos?)

1871
01:07:50,484 --> 01:07:52,384
(Today's B-Side is seen when you zoom out.)

1872
01:07:52,854 --> 01:07:55,924
(The first photo: A haenyeo's buoy)

1873
01:07:56,384 --> 01:07:58,624
We take the buoy which is with the fishnet.

1874
01:07:58,894 --> 01:08:01,464
It's like a lifeline. Without it, we'd die.

1875
01:08:01,664 --> 01:08:04,164
We can't dive without it.

1876
01:08:04,164 --> 01:08:06,064
(A haenyeo's lifeline in the vast open sea, a buoy)

1877
01:08:06,064 --> 01:08:07,964
(What's the secret behind the water droplet photo?)

1878
01:08:08,204 --> 01:08:10,164
(The second photo: A haenyeo's diving goggles)

1879
01:08:10,164 --> 01:08:11,474
The goggles.

1880
01:08:11,834 --> 01:08:14,504
In the Jeju dialect, diving goggles are called eyes.

1881
01:08:15,174 --> 01:08:18,044
You can't catch anything without goggles.

1882
01:08:18,044 --> 01:08:20,014
You have to see underwater with them.

1883
01:08:20,244 --> 01:08:23,484
(Jeju haenyeo have lived and learned the ways of the sea through them.)

1884
01:08:23,754 --> 01:08:26,684
(And what is the final photo?)

1885
01:08:27,484 --> 01:08:30,224
(A haenyeo's hands)

1886
01:08:30,224 --> 01:08:31,324
Really?

1887
01:08:32,154 --> 01:08:33,154
Their hands.

1888
01:08:33,154 --> 01:08:37,034
(They were the hands of the haenyeo.)

1889
01:08:37,294 --> 01:08:41,764
(The real treasure we were meant to find)

1890
01:08:42,804 --> 01:08:44,234
About 70 percent...

1891
01:08:44,234 --> 01:08:46,834
of women in Jeju worked in the sea.

1892
01:08:46,934 --> 01:08:49,844
After diving for 50 or 60 years,

1893
01:08:49,844 --> 01:08:51,714
I'm almost 80 now,

1894
01:08:51,714 --> 01:08:53,344
and I'm still at it.

1895
01:08:54,814 --> 01:08:57,514
(Spending all those years in the sea,)

1896
01:08:57,514 --> 01:09:00,184
(from a blooming young girl to a grandmother)

1897
01:09:01,324 --> 01:09:03,284
It was because it was the path I chose,

1898
01:09:03,284 --> 01:09:05,824
and what I did best.

1899
01:09:07,224 --> 01:09:09,524
Over 45 years of diving,

1900
01:09:09,994 --> 01:09:11,434
there's nothing I haven't done.

1901
01:09:12,064 --> 01:09:13,904
I raised my three sons...

1902
01:09:13,904 --> 01:09:15,364
without ever letting them shed a tear,

1903
01:09:16,104 --> 01:09:19,004
put them through school, and even got them all married off.

1904
01:09:19,374 --> 01:09:20,644
Going through all that...

1905
01:09:22,244 --> 01:09:24,544
I'm getting so choked up I can barely speak.

1906
01:09:26,344 --> 01:09:27,714
I raised my three sons,

1907
01:09:28,684 --> 01:09:31,254
put them through school, and even got them all married off.

1908
01:09:31,584 --> 01:09:33,854
They're really admirable.

1909
01:09:36,324 --> 01:09:39,194
The sea, too, has been protected because we were here.

1910
01:09:40,024 --> 01:09:41,664
That's how we've been living our lives.

1911
01:09:42,394 --> 01:09:44,064
(The haenyeo who have protected that sea are still right here.)

1912
01:09:44,064 --> 01:09:45,234
Right.

1913
01:09:45,234 --> 01:09:47,164
(The haenyeo who have protected that sea are still right here.)

1914
01:09:48,164 --> 01:09:52,974
(Their lives, lived with the ocean, are a treasure in themselves.)

1915
01:09:53,434 --> 01:09:56,574
(And...)

1916
01:09:57,944 --> 01:10:00,044
(A poem our haenyeo wants to share with us)

1917
01:10:00,044 --> 01:10:02,684
"Diary of an Old Haenyeo."

1918
01:10:03,644 --> 01:10:05,284
Oh Sun Rye.

1919
01:10:05,814 --> 01:10:08,224
"The crashing sound of the waves is..."

1920
01:10:08,224 --> 01:10:10,654
"the wailing of a grieving woman."

1921
01:10:11,594 --> 01:10:14,994
"They say the seawater is made of a haenyeo's tears."

1922
01:10:15,264 --> 01:10:18,534
"Ten fathoms down, ten fathoms back up."

1923
01:10:19,064 --> 01:10:21,264
"Holding my breath for 20 fathoms."

1924
01:10:22,834 --> 01:10:24,474
"Letting out a whistling breath."

1925
01:10:25,004 --> 01:10:26,904
"Still, I must live on."

1926
01:10:27,574 --> 01:10:30,204
"They say this world is better than the next."

1927
01:10:31,144 --> 01:10:35,584
"Everyone, the tide is right. Let's go diving."

1928
01:10:36,014 --> 01:10:40,014
"The top shells and abalones are calling us to hurry over."

1929
01:10:40,314 --> 01:10:44,654
"Let's go make some money. Let's go earn our living."

1930
01:10:45,154 --> 01:10:46,394
Thank you.

1931
01:10:46,394 --> 01:10:49,724
(Thank you.)

1932
01:11:01,244 --> 01:11:04,344
(Thank you so much!)

1933
01:11:04,644 --> 01:11:06,044
So today's B-Side was...

1934
01:11:06,614 --> 01:11:08,784
(It was haenyeo.)

1935
01:11:08,784 --> 01:11:10,214
- This was it.
- I never thought of it.

1936
01:11:10,484 --> 01:11:12,314
- I never thought of it.
- I know.

1937
01:11:12,484 --> 01:11:14,254
The haenyeo's restaurant.

1938
01:11:14,984 --> 01:11:16,024
Number one was the answer.

1939
01:11:16,024 --> 01:11:18,054
So they didn't catch the octopus today, right?

1940
01:11:18,454 --> 01:11:19,524
We bought it.

1941
01:11:19,524 --> 01:11:21,524
(We bought it.)

1942
01:11:21,724 --> 01:11:24,234
No wonder it tasted different from the one my dad caught.

1943
01:11:24,634 --> 01:11:26,334
I wanted to say that so badly.

1944
01:11:26,334 --> 01:11:27,404
But just in case...

1945
01:11:27,634 --> 01:11:29,964
- The octopus that had pee?
- Yes.

1946
01:11:30,304 --> 01:11:32,034
- It's different.
- It's "Nyocchi," just like gnocchi.

1947
01:11:32,034 --> 01:11:34,744
(Nyocchi)

1948
01:11:34,744 --> 01:11:36,644
I had so much fun today.

1949
01:11:36,644 --> 01:11:39,244
I was so happy to join the last episode.

1950
01:11:39,874 --> 01:11:43,544
We got to wrap up the last episode of the season...

1951
01:11:43,544 --> 01:11:45,154
on Jeju Island.

1952
01:11:45,154 --> 01:11:46,354
I had so much fun.

1953
01:11:46,354 --> 01:11:48,254
It ends so fast. It's a shame.

1954
01:11:48,754 --> 01:11:51,524
I'm too busy to go outside.

1955
01:11:51,524 --> 01:11:53,494
Thanks to Sixth Sense, I got to go around a lot,

1956
01:11:53,494 --> 01:11:55,824
try something new, and see.

1957
01:11:55,824 --> 01:11:57,834
I also got to spend time with young people.

1958
01:11:58,634 --> 01:12:01,834
- It was the best time ever.
- Good.

1959
01:12:02,364 --> 01:12:05,604
Thanks to everyone who joined our show.

1960
01:12:05,604 --> 01:12:09,044
Most of all, thanks to the viewers for all their support.

1961
01:12:09,044 --> 01:12:10,974
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1962
01:12:10,974 --> 01:12:12,474
(Thank you for watching.)

1963
01:12:12,474 --> 01:12:13,474
Thank you.
